Creating a point system to predict the likelihood of an MLB batter not hitting a double in a game involves evaluating various factors that influence double-hitting probabilities. You've identified several key categories:

Current Streak: The batter's recent performance, particularly their streak of games with or without doubles.​

Average Streak: The typical length of games in which the batter records doubles, providing insight into their consistency.​

Stadium: The ballpark's dimensions and characteristics can significantly affect extra-base hits.​

Time of Day: Day or night games can influence visibility and playing conditions, potentially impacting performance.​

Starting Pitcher: The opposing pitcher's skill level, pitching style, and handedness can affect a batter's success.​

Average Doubles per At-Bat: The batter's historical rate of hitting doubles per at-bat. For example, Pete Scott had the highest career doubles per at-bat ratio at 0.08. ​
